WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.960
Tesla's first American-made LFP cells are now powering megapacks rolling out of the factory, tightening the domestic supply chain for utility scale storage. This is Tesla Shorts Time, episode 559. Tesla has started deliveries of megapacks that use LFP cells produced in the United States. The move reduces reliance on imported battery chemistry for large-scale energy projects. It also aligns production with domestic content incentives that

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:57.740
affect project economics for utilities. The first units mark a shift toward localized cell manufacturing for the energy storage side of the business. Production of these cells supports broader efforts to meet demand from utilities seeking domestic content credits. The change comes as Tesla scales megapack output to match growing grid storage orders. Now the focus turns to how Tesla is expanding its vehicle lineup for different markets.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:26.960
The extended wheelbase Model YL is now available for order in the United States. The variant adds rear leg room and cargo space compared with the standard model. Production is tied to existing lines rather than a separate facility. Customers in India can now schedule test drives of the Model Y premium rear wheel drive variant. The launch expands the Model Y lineup for buyers seeking more interior room without moving to a different vehicle class.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:49.800
Both variants draw from current assembly capacity serving their respective regions. Vehicle hardware updates lead naturally into software changes rolling out to cars already on the road. The same software update is pushing GROC AI into vehicles already on the road. The rollout pairs a new trim option with an expanded in-car assistant feature.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:02:03.200
Production of the premium RWD version ties into existing assembly lines serving the Indian market. The summer update adds a zoom control that lets drivers adjust the scale of the on-screen camera view.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:14.740
Users can lock in a preferred distance for surrounding traffic display. The option gives owners direct control over how much of the environment appears on the screen during supervised driving.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:28.060
The feature appears in the visualization settings and works alongside existing camera feeds. Tesla Update 2026.20 brings several adjustments that owners have documented beyond the official release notes.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:34.760
The changes include refinements to interface behavior identified through direct testing after installation.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:41.380
Observers have compiled a list of differences between the published notes and what actually appears in the vehicles.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:54.340
Interface tweaks form the main category of updates that affect routine interactions with the car. The release continues the pattern of incremental improvements that reach cars without requiring a service visit.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:01.800
Vehicle software refinements set up a closer look at how supervised FSD is actually performing on public roads.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:10.180
Owners report that the latest supervised FSD version sometimes cancels turn signals about 100 feet before a turn.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:19.240
The same build also drives over elevated road sections at full speed instead of slowing, producing a hard landing on the far side.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:29.600
Drivers say they now intervene when they see those features ahead. The reports come from real world testing on roads with both signal timing quirks and uneven pavement.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:39.580
An owner reported that supervised FSD managed the post-concert traffic jam at a large venue, completing the exit in roughly 90 minutes.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:49.840
Occasional accelerator nudges were needed in the densest sections. The drive showed both the system's ability to handle dense, chaotic flow and its remaining limits.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:58.400
The same driver described FSD handling the approach and entry into the Lincoln tunnel on i95 without intervention.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:08.820
Lane changes and merging occurred smoothly in heavy traffic. The account adds to the growing set of highway specific owner reports for the current software version.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:14.240
Performance questions on public roads connect to a recent high speed incident under review.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:22.000
A Tesla operating in supervised full self-driving mode lost its entire rear section in a crash in Isakwa.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:27.580
The incident is under review for how the vehicle responded in the moments before impact.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:34.540
Tesla has not released details on whether the driver had hands on the wheel or issued any override commands.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:41.820
The crash raises questions about structural performance in high speed rear impacts while the system was engaged.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:49.140
Hardware and safety considerations shift focus to longer-term business model changes around FSD access.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:57.840
Subscription access to supervised FSD changes the timing of revenue recognition in ways that traditional vehicle sales never did.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:08.900
Instead of booking most of the margin at delivery, Tesla can now spread payments across months or years, while the same hardware continues to generate incremental service revenue.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:16.860
That shift lowers the capital intensity of each new customer because the car is already built and the software is already written.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:26.140
The model also creates a feedback loop where usage data directly informs further software development without requiring additional hardware sales.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:33.140
The $99 monthly plan also alters the risk profile for buyers who are unsure about the technology.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:42.180
A driver can test the system without committing to a large upfront purchase, which widens the pool of potential users beyond early adopters.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:51.320
At the same time, the recurring stream gives Tesla visibility into future cash flows that can be modeled against development and regulatory costs.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:59.640
Retention becomes the central variable because owners can cancel at any time if the supervised experience falls short of expectations.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:08.520
If adoption scales, the model creates a margin layer that is less exposed to raw material price swings or factory utilization rates.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:19.000
The open question is whether enough owners will keep the subscription active once the novelty wears off and the remaining limitations of supervised operation become familiar.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:27.980
That retention rate will determine whether the program becomes a durable profit center or a temporary bridge to full unsupervised capability.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:33.640
Recurring revenue thinking leads into institutional capital flowing into the stock.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:47.280
Aberdeen Group added 800 3,088 shares during the second quarter, bringing its total to 5,109,041 shares valued at $2.149 billion.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.400
The position now represents 3.0172% of the firm's overall portfolio.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:07:00.580
The purchase was executed at a reported average price of $420.60.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:06.620
The increase shows continued institutional interest from a major UK wealth manager.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:13.200
Jim Kramer told viewers that conviction in Elon Musk remains the key variable for Tesla's valuation.

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:19.380
He framed the company's multiple business lines as still tied to Musk's execution track record.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:24.660
The comment came amid ongoing questions about regulatory and competitive pressure.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:31.060
Kramer positioned the stock as one where belief in leadership outweighs near-term operational metrics.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:40.280
Analysts mapped overlapping manufacturing, supply chain and capital allocation advantages that could arise from combining Tesla and SpaceX.

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:46.920
The note highlights shared use of advanced materials and production techniques already proven at scale.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:50.860
No formal process has been confirmed by either firm.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:56.820
The discussion centers on potential efficiencies in engineering talent and facility utilization.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:05.800
Jean Munster argued that operational overlap in talent, facilities and long-term capital projects supports a merged structure.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:13.220
He pointed to shared engineering culture and the ability to move resources between vehicle and aerospace programs.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:18.520
The view treats the idea as strategically coherent rather than speculative.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:23.780
Munster emphasized common approaches to scaling complex hardware systems.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:35.340
A recent analysis examined whether any current EV maker has replicated Tesla's combination of software iteration, vertical integration and energy storage growth.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.140
It contrasts balance sheet strength and production discipline across the sector.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:43.600
No single company is named as the clear successor.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:51.100
The analysis focuses on how few peers match Tesla's pace of over-the-air updates and storage deployment.

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:57.380
Institutional moves sit alongside Tesla's own efforts to shape policy and protect IP.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:05.420
Tesla filed a petition asking the Supreme Court to clarify how the USPTP should handle certain review proceedings.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:10.860
The request targets policies that affect how patent challenges move through the agency.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:17.060
A favorable ruling could change the timeline and cost of defending Tesla's intellectual property.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:24.460
The filing reflects growing complexity in protecting hardware and software innovations across vehicle and energy lines.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:31.420
Tesla Energy registered lobbying activity at Queens Park focused on energy storage and grid projects.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:36.900
The filing lists meetings with provincial officials on permitting and procurement rules.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:42.720
The activity shows Tesla's continued push to expand megapack deployments in Ontario.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.480
The registration covers discussions around storage incentives and interconnection processes.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:54.160
Policy work at home and abroad contrasts with everyday owner experiences on the road.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:10:01.920
A mother and daughter completed a 5,000-mile cross-country trip relying on standard household outlets for overnight charging.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:07.980
The model Y handled the full distance without dedicated fast charging stops after dark.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:14.540
The account shows how level one charging can support long distance travel when daily mileage stays moderate.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:20.800
The trip relied on consistent access to 110 volt outlets at each overnight stop.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:28.620
The experience demonstrates practical use cases for owners without access to home or public fast chargers on every leg.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:39.480
Before we go, the Supreme Court petition on patent review timelines will be worth watching for any early signals on how it might affect Tesla's broader intellectual property strategy.

